# --- Scan output: NEVER COMMIT -------------------------------------------
# MORF extracts credentials from real apps, so everything it writes is a
# secret-bearing artifact. Committing its output turns this repository into the
# kind of leak it exists to find. These patterns are generic on purpose: naming
# individual output files both misses the next one and records which artifact
# was scanned.
#
# This is a backstop, not the control — a new output filename is caught by
# neither this file nor a scanner. Before committing, check:
#   git diff --cached --name-only | grep -niE '(^|/)(results|backup)/|_scan|_triage|_unmasked|\.(apk|ipa|aab|sarif)$'
#
# Fabricated test fixtures ARE allowed and required: a secret scanner's test
# suite cannot run without credential-shaped data. The line is PROVENANCE, not
# shape — a value you invented is a fixture, a value a scan handed you is not.
# Anchored to the repo root with a leading slash: an unanchored "backup/" also
# matches the Go package morf/backup/, and "results/" would match any nested
# dir of that name. The scan-output dirs this is about live at the root.
/results/
/backup/
/output/
*_scan.json
*_scan_unmasked.json
*_unmasked.json
*_triage.json
*.sarif

# --- Real app artifacts: NEVER COMMIT ------------------------------------
# Negations below keep the deliberate test fixtures tracked. Adding a NEW
# artifact under these paths is a conscious act and needs its own negation —
# that friction is the point.
*.apk
*.ipa
*.aab
!morf/ios/testdata/*.ipa
!morf/benchmark/corpus/**/*.ipa
!morf/benchmark/corpus/**/*.apk

# Benchmark corpus fixtures are test data, not build artifacts — must be tracked.
!morf/benchmark/corpus/**/*.so

# apktool.jar is fetched on demand (scripts/fetch-tools.sh), not committed.
morf/tools/apktool.jar
